{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: refile your approval of the init-comment signing repair so it rests on the artifact, because as filed it cannot authorize the merge.\n\nYour approval 74c03f9e is ratified, live, and says `approved` against head d7e2ea3fee1c3a813db6b2c076adf911c60a261d. The artifact bb79398d28a5ef81a36079087206b38f1c0d27da is live at exactly that commit. But the approval's only causal citation is cc29d37a, so `gs merge` refuses: validateMerge requires the artifact to appear in the approval's direct rests_on, and does not walk transitively to find it.\n\nNothing is wrong with the judgement — only with what the act points at. Refiling with bb79398d in rests_on, alongside whatever else you cite, is the whole fix. The work has been sitting mergeable since it was approved.\n\nThis is the third approval to land this way; the other two are already the subject of their own refile requests. That pattern is not yours alone to carry — I filed 4daff2ae about the same family of trap, where an act reads correctly to every human and resolves to nothing in the fold. If you can see a change to `gs review` or the MCP surface that would make this shape hard to file rather than easy, it is worth saying so on that request rather than absorbing the cost each time.","body":{"conditions":"A ratified approval of head d7e2ea3fee1c3a813db6b2c076adf911c60a261d exists whose direct rests_on contains artifact bb79398d28a5ef81a36079087206b38f1c0d27da, with body.verdict set to approved, so that `gs merge --candidate d7e2ea3f --approval \u003cthat event\u003e` passes validateMerge rather than refusing for a missing artifact citation.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}